{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-20 11:08:56 GMT  \nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-20 18:08:56 +07 (Indochina Time)\n\n### Q1 — unknown-evidence silence tolerance\n\n**RULING: Keep the stricter literal rule: no tolerance after a gap.**\n\nThe contract deliberately distinguishes continuous observation from incomplete evidence. After `:dao.stream/gap`, the lease is `unknown`; absence is expressly not evidence beyond the retained observation window, and older renewals cannot be inferred as newest. Applying tolerance would extend tenure precisely when the judge has weaker evidence. The holder-favoring bias supports tolerance only where observation continuity justifies it; it does not require granting grace on uncertain evidence.\n\nNo contract amendment is required. Retain:\n\n> “A lease whose evidence state is `unknown` is due for `:silence` only once a full duration has passed since its resumed reading.”\n\n### Q2 — holder cap-basis flight time\n\n**RULING: Accept the flight-time gap; do not discount estimated flight time.**\n\nThe judge’s cap is measured from grant tenure start, while the holder’s bound begins when it observes the grant. That asymmetry is real, but the holder contract explicitly says the bound “bounds attention, not access”; exclusion and fencing belong to the resource. Requiring the holder to estimate and subtract grant flight time would introduce an assumption the holder may not be able to establish reliably and would complicate the possession protocol. The judge may reclaim at cap while a holder acts during the observation-flight window; resource fencing must make that safe.\n\nNo contract amendment is required. Retain:\n\n> “The bound bounds attention, not access; a holder needing exclusion obtains it from the resource.”\n\n### Q3 — served-endpoint latency\n\n**RULING: Accept the latency as composition policy.**\n\nThe serving step has mandatory endpoint work and already polls each medium once per tick. Adding a probe would duplicate reads without deleting the existing poll, violating the adoption rule that a migration must delete what it replaces. The serving inbound path has no legitimate nudge caller, so the resulting first-request delay—up to the configured 200 ms ceiling—is an explicit tradeoff rather than a waitset correctness defect.\n\nThe plan should make the decision explicit by replacing the tentative wording with:\n\n> “This served-composition first-request latency, up to the configured backoff ceiling (200 ms in the default composition), is accepted as composition policy; a composition requiring lower latency must lower its ceiling or provide a serving-path nudge.”\n\nNo change to the governing stream or lease contract is needed.\n\nQ1: stricter literal no-tolerance-after-gap | Q2: accept flight-time gap; resource fencing owns exclusion | Q3: accept latency as composition policy, with explicit plan wording"}}
